This file describes the file system used for all game files, in src/libs/io.

		       THE UQM FILE SYSTEM
		       -------------------

The game reads and writes files through a virtual file system. It stacks
host directories and zip archives on top of each other, so that addons can
replace content files without changing them.

Sources
-------

The implementation is in src/libs/io/standard and uses std::filesystem,
std::filebuf and zlib. libs/io/FileSystem.h makes uqm::io::FileSystem,
File, Directory and Mount aliases for its types.

Each mount has a MountSource (standard/MountSource.h): DirectorySource for
a host directory and ZipSource for a zip archive. A new kind of storage,
for example files in memory, is a new MountSource.

Types
-----

FileSystem  Owns the mount stack. The game has one, gameFileSystem() in
            options.h, from initIO() to uninitIO(). Its directories are
            contentDir(), configDir(), saveDir(), meleeDir() and scrShotDir().
Directory   A path in a file system. It is a value type and can be copied.
            It keeps the mounts alive, so it can outlive the FileSystem.
File        An open file. It is move-only and closes the file when it is
            destroyed. It is buffered, and supports unget() of bytes that
            are still in the buffer.
Mount       A mount in the stack. It is move-only and removes the mount
            when it is destroyed. It can outlive the FileSystem.
Matcher     A file name filter for Directory::list(): literal, prefix,
            suffix, substring or regular expression.

Functions that can fail return an empty optional or an error_code. They
do not throw and do not set errno.

Mounts
------

FileSystem::mountDirectory() mounts a host directory.
FileSystem::mountArchive() mounts a zip archive that is a file in the
file system. Archives are always read-only. Stored and deflated entries are
supported. Zip64 and encrypted entries are not.
FileSystem::mountTransplant() mounts what a directory currently shows at a
different mount point. The game uses it for addon "shadow-content".

Each mount goes at the top or bottom of the stack, or directly above or
below a mount that is given as the relative mount.

Rules for the mount stack
-------------------------

- A higher mount hides the entries of a lower mount with the same name.
  A directory can hide a file, and a file can hide a directory.
- Reading a file opens the highest entry.
- Directory listings contain the entries of all mounts, without
  duplicates, sorted by name. Mount points show as directories.
- A new file or directory goes into the highest writable mount that
  contains the path. The parent directories are created in that mount.
  The parent directory must exist in the file system.
- Opening a file for writing that exists only in a read-only mount first
  copies it to the highest writable mount above that mount. If there is no
  such mount, the open fails.
- Removing an entry removes it from all mounts that have it. It fails at
  the first read-only mount that has the entry.
- A rename must stay in one mount.

Threads
-------

A FileSystem can be used from many threads. A File must be used by one
thread at a time. Mounting and unmounting can happen while other threads
read files.

Old code
--------

The resource functions in libs/reslib.h (res_OpenResFile, ReadResFile and
so on) are thin functions over File. Code that keeps a file in a structure
that is allocated as C memory owns it as uqgsl::owner<uqm::io::File*>.
